icant political force in Canada on August 3, 1961, following the merger of various socialist and labor-oriented groups, including the Co-operative Commonwealth Federation (CCF). Centered in Ottawa, the NDP has played a pivotal role in shaping Canadian social policy and labor rights, reflecting a broader global shift towards progressive politics during the mid-20th century. Figures such as Tommy Douglas, often celebrated as the 'father of Medicare', and Ed Broadbent, who led the party during its rise in the 1980s, helped cement the NDP's identity as a champion of social justice and equality.

